# Installed packages. Reproducible from package.json + package-lock.json, and large
# enough that committing it makes every diff unreadable.
node_modules/

# Real secrets. Two lines, not one, and the distinction matters:
#
#   .env      the actual file, holding the actual JWT_SECRET
#   .env.*    variants such as .env.local, .env.production
#
# The original .gitignore had ONLY `.env.*`. That pattern does not match `.env` —
# the `.*` requires a literal dot and at least one character after it. So the one
# file that actually contains a secret was the one file not ignored, and a routine
# `git add .` would have published it. A near-miss like this is worth a comment
# rather than a silent fix, because the same mistake reads as correct at a glance.
#
# `.env.example` is deliberately NOT ignored (see the negation below): it carries
# placeholders only, and it is the file that tells a new cloner what to set.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Runtime and tooling output
coverage/
*.log
npm-debug.log*

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
